## Why this topic matters

The Equality Act 2010 is the Act that bites whenever a digital scenario
involves a decision about a person, where that decision can be linked
to one of the nine protected characteristics. That covers job adverts
worded to exclude older applicants, recruitment AI trained on biased
historical data, customer-service flows that fail older or disabled
users, and access to goods and services delivered through digital
products.

Many of these scenarios will not look like "equality" cases at first
glance because the bias is implicit in a technical choice rather than
explicit in a decision-maker's words. Paper 1 rewards students who can
see through the surface neutrality of an algorithm or a process and
identify the protected characteristic that is being disadvantaged.

## What the law actually is

The Equality Act 2010 is the consolidating statute that replaced nine
earlier pieces of UK anti-discrimination legislation, including the
Race Relations Act 1976, the Sex Discrimination Act 1975, the
Disability Discrimination Act 1995 and the Equal Pay Act 1970. The
2010 Act brings these strands into one statute and adds protections
that the earlier law did not contain.

The Act covers protection in employment (Part 5), services and public
functions (Part 3), premises (Part 4), education (Part 6) and
associations (Part 7). For digital scenarios the most common parts
engaged are employment (covering recruitment processes, AI screening,
workplace systems and post-employment matters such as references) and
services and public functions (any digital product
offered to customers or the public).

The Act also imposes the Public Sector Equality Duty under section 149.
This duty requires public authorities (and bodies exercising public
functions) to have due regard to eliminating discrimination, advancing
equality of opportunity, and fostering good relations between people
who share a protected characteristic and those who do not. The PSED is
the reason equality impact assessments are routinely required when
public bodies procure or build digital systems.

## The nine protected characteristics

Section 4 of the Act lists nine protected characteristics. Memorising
them as a set is the foundation of every Equality Act answer, because
the first move in any scenario is to identify which characteristic is
engaged.

1. **Age**
2. **Disability**
3. **Gender reassignment**
4. **Marriage and civil partnership**
5. **Pregnancy and maternity**
6. **Race**, defined to include colour, nationality and ethnic or
   national origin
7. **Religion or belief**, including the absence of religion or belief
8. **Sex**
9. **Sexual orientation**

Some characteristics have nuance. Age can be defined by a specific age
or by an age range. Disability has a statutory definition under section
6 that requires a physical or mental impairment with a substantial,
long-term and adverse effect on normal day-to-day activities. Race
covers nationality and ethnic origin as well as colour. Religion or
belief covers a lack of religion or belief.

## The forms of prohibited conduct

The Act lists several distinct ways in which conduct can be unlawful.
Recognising which one applies to a scenario is the second move after
identifying the characteristic.

### Direct discrimination (section 13)

A person treats another less favourably because of a protected
characteristic. The "because of" is wide: it covers both characteristics
the victim actually has and characteristics the victim is perceived
to have, and it covers situations where the less favourable treatment
arises because of someone the victim is associated with. Direct
discrimination usually cannot be justified, with one important
exception: direct age discrimination can be justified if the
discrimination is a proportionate means of achieving a legitimate aim.

### Indirect discrimination (section 19)

A "provision, criterion or practice" (PCP) is applied to everyone, but
it puts persons sharing a protected characteristic at a particular
disadvantage compared with others, and the person to whom the PCP is
applied is themselves disadvantaged. Indirect discrimination is the
form that catches surface-neutral rules: dress codes that
disproportionately affect women, fitness tests that disproportionately
affect older workers, voice-recognition systems that disproportionately
affect users with strong regional accents.

Indirect discrimination is defensible if the PCP is a proportionate
means of achieving a legitimate aim. The burden of proving this falls
on the party applying the PCP, and the test is whether the aim is real
and serious, and whether the PCP is no more discriminatory than is
necessary to achieve it. Convenience alone almost never meets the
test.

### Harassment (section 26)

Unwanted conduct related to a protected characteristic that has the
purpose or effect of violating dignity or creating an intimidating,
hostile, degrading, humiliating or offensive environment. Harassment
does not require malice on the part of the perpetrator; the effect on
the victim is what counts (assessed objectively given the
circumstances).

### Victimisation (section 27)

Subjecting a person to a detriment because they have done, or are
suspected of doing, a protected act. Protected acts include bringing
proceedings under the Act, giving evidence in such proceedings, making
an allegation that someone has contravened the Act, and doing anything
else for the purposes of or in connection with the Act.
Victimisation can be committed by anyone in any capacity.

## The disability-specific duty: reasonable adjustments

Sections 20 to 22 impose a positive duty on employers and service
providers to make reasonable adjustments where a provision, a physical
feature, or the absence of an auxiliary aid puts a disabled person at
a substantial disadvantage compared to non-disabled people. The duty
is anticipatory in the services context: it is owed before any
specific disabled customer turns up, so the service must be designed
with accessibility built in.

Failure to comply with the reasonable adjustments duty is itself
unlawful discrimination under section 21. For digital products this
duty most often surfaces as a requirement to provide alternative
input methods (text alongside voice, keyboard alongside mouse), to
support screen readers, to meet contrast and font-scaling standards,
and to offer a non-digital fallback for users who cannot complete the
digital flow.

## Where the Act applies in digital scenarios

For Paper 1, two parts of the Act are dominant.

**Employment (Part 5).** Covers recruitment (job adverts, shortlisting,
application processes, interview decisions), terms of employment,
promotion,
training, dismissal, and post-employment situations such as
references. Algorithmic recruitment screening that filters CVs against
historical hiring patterns is the textbook example: even when the
algorithm is not given the protected characteristic as a feature, it
can reproduce the bias of the historical data.

**Services and public functions (Part 3).** Covers any service offered
to the public or a section of the public, including services offered
by digital products. This is where most consumer-facing digital
discrimination cases sit. Banking, retail, customer service,
ticketing, education platforms and government digital services all
fall under Part 3.

## Enforcement and consequences

The Equality and Human Rights Commission (EHRC) is the regulator. It
can carry out formal investigations, issue unlawful-act notices, enter
into agreements with bodies that have breached the Act, and bring
judicial review proceedings on questions of public-sector equality
duty.

Individuals affected by discrimination can also bring their own
claims. Employment cases go to the Employment Tribunal. Services and
goods cases go to the County Court under section 114. Remedies
include declarations, injunctions requiring changes to processes or
products, orders to take specific steps to remedy the discrimination,
and damages including damages for injury to feelings (which have
established bands). There is no statutory cap on Equality Act damages.

For public bodies, breach of the Public Sector Equality Duty under
section 149 is grounds for judicial review. Procurement decisions and
major digital deployments by public bodies are routinely challenged on
PSED grounds.

## What Paper 1 expects

When a Paper 1 scenario shows a decision, process or product that
disadvantages a group, the path through the answer is:

1. **Name the Act and the characteristic.** "The Equality Act 2010,
   on the grounds of [age / disability / sex / race / ...]." Where the
   scenario engages more than one characteristic, name the most central
   one and note any others.

2. **Explain the form of discrimination.** State whether the conduct
   is direct (section 13), indirect (section 19), harassment (section
   26), victimisation (section 27) or a failure to make reasonable
   adjustments (section 21). Define the relevant test in your own
   words.

3. **Apply the test to the scenario.** Tie the elements of the
   relevant section to the facts. For indirect discrimination, identify
   the PCP and the disadvantaged group, and assess whether the
   objective justification defence is plausible on the facts.

The recurring trap is the surface-neutral algorithm or process. A
process that does not mention a protected characteristic on its face
can still be unlawful if it puts a group sharing that characteristic
at a particular disadvantage. The strongest Paper 1 answers spell out
the PCP, name the disadvantage and assess justification on its merits
rather than accepting the defendant's framing.

The worked examples in the Method section show this path running on
past-paper questions. Once you have practised the assessment below,
come back to those.
